## INPUT METHODS

Scenario: getInputIntTest1 | User enters an integer within the specified range
GIVEN user calls method TerminalView.getInputInt() with minimum 1, maximum 5, and input "3"
WHEN user enters the integer 3
THEN the method returns 3

Scenario: getInputIntTest2 | User enters an integer without a specified range
GIVEN user calls method TerminalView.getInputInt() with input "25"
WHEN user enters the integer 25
THEN the method returns 25

Scenario: getInputIntTest3 | User enters an integer outside the specified range
GIVEN user calls method TerminalView.getInputInt() with minimum 1, maximum 5, and inputs "8" and "3"
WHEN user first enters 8, followed by 3
THEN the method rejects 8 and returns 3

Scenario: getInputStrTest1 | User enters a string matching a specified regular expression
GIVEN user calls method TerminalView.getInputStr() with regular expression "\\d+" and input "12345"
WHEN user enters the string "12345"
THEN the method returns "12345"

Scenario: getInputStrTest2 | User enters a string without a specified regular expression
GIVEN user calls method TerminalView.getInputStr() with input "username"
WHEN user enters the string "username"
THEN the method returns "username"

Scenario: getInputStrTest3 | User enters a string that initially does not match the specified regular expression
GIVEN user calls method TerminalView.getInputStr() with regular expression "\\d+" and inputs "abc" and "123"
WHEN user first enters "abc", followed by "123"
THEN the method rejects "abc" and returns "123"

Scenario: promptMessageTest1 | User displays a prompt message
GIVEN user calls method TerminalView.promptMessage() with message "Test message"
WHEN the message is displayed
THEN the output contains "Test message" followed by a line separator

